  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>For questions 1 – 4, use the following scenario to use equations to solve a riddle:</para>
    <blockquote>Here is a riddle: “I am thinking of two numbers that add up to 5.678. The difference between them is 9.876. What are the two numbers?”</blockquote>
    <para>Name any pair of numbers whose sum is 5.678.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>2 and 3.678 </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Name any pair of numbers whose difference is 9.876.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>10 and 0.124 </para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>The riddle can be represented with two equations. Write the equations.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>Let \(x\) and \(y\) represent the numbers: \(x+y=5.678\) and \(x-y=9.876\).</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
<!--Text Entry Interaction Start -->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Solve the riddle. Be prepared to show your reasoning.</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer: Your answer may vary, but here is a sample.</para>
    <para>The two numbers are \(7.777\) and \(-2.099\).</para>
    <list>
      <item>Adding the two equations eliminates the \(y\)-variable and gives \(2x=15.554\) or \(x=7.777\).</item>
      <item> Substituting this \(x\)-value into either equation gives \(y=-2.099\).</item>
      <item>Graphing the two equations gives two lines that intersect at \((7.777,−2.099)\).</item>
    </list>
    <figure><media alt="Graph of 2 intersecting lines. Horizontal axis, labeled x, from negative 12 to 12 by 2s. Vertical axis, labeled y, from negative 12 to 12 by 2s. Lines intersect near 8 comma negative 2."><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/7e257c4f412c999470ad160806aa1d6b563dac7b"/></media></figure></solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->
</section>
